# tmpfiles.d(5) lines, applied by systemd-tmpfiles-setup.service every boot.
# The general-purpose lever for anything with no credential of its own.
# `f+` truncates and rewrites, so it overrides a file baked into the image;
# plain `f` only creates a missing one.

# Hostname. systemd 259 has no firstboot.hostname/system.hostname credential,
# so this is how a generic image gets a per-host name.
f+ /etc/hostname 0644 root root - nas-01

# A user, declared as a myosi identity record rather than created directly.
#
# Do NOT use home.create.<name> or sysusers.extra for this. Both bypass
# /usr/libexec/myosi/user-provision, so the account ends up with no
# subuid/subgid range (rootless podman broken), no sysext group bindings and
# no linger — and user-sweep never adopts it, because it only scans
# /etc/myosi/users/ and /usr/share/myosi/users/. home.create is worse still:
# systemd 259 rejects a record carrying a `secret` section outright, and
# without one homectl blocks on an interactive password prompt.
#
# Writing the record instead lets myosi-users.service create the account on
# the same boot, through the path that already gets the storage flags right
# (luks-offline-discard=no, auto-resize-mode=grow, the SELinux defcontext,
# a disk size computed from free space) and that allocates subuid/subgid,
# binds sysext groups and sets linger.
#
# Drop "service":"io.systemd.Home" for a classic user — the right choice on a
# headless host, where an encrypted home never unlocks and linger plus
# rootless quadlets are what you actually want.
f+ /etc/myosi/users/alan.user 0644 root root - {"userName":"alan","uid":1000,"gid":1000,"realName":"Alan","homeDirectory":"/home/alan","shell":"/usr/bin/fish","memberOf":["wheel","video","render","input","kvm"],"service":"io.systemd.Home"}

# Linger for a classic user, if you did not let user-provision set it.
#f /var/lib/systemd/linger/alan 0644 root root -

# A NetworkManager keyfile — myosi has no networkd for the network.network.*
# credentials to reach. Mode MUST be 0600 or NM ignores it. Escape newlines
# as \n; the argument is a single tmpfiles line.
#f+ /etc/NetworkManager/system-connections/lan.nmconnection 0600 root root - [connection]\nid=lan\ntype=ethernet\ninterface-name=enp1s0\n\n[ipv4]\nmethod=manual\naddress1=10.0.0.20/24,10.0.0.1\ndns=10.0.0.1\n\n[ipv6]\nmethod=disabled\n
